From 6ece7115d354facf8412c65797a85ab2bf667c2e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <plab2090@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 23 Jul 2026 02:51:37 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 12/12] npu: react to a model pick immediately, and show every
 loaded model
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Picking a model in /model left the LED red for up to ten seconds. The
preload request went out and the router accepted it, but the client kept
the idle poll it had already scheduled, so nothing on screen moved until
that timer fired. Long enough that the model looked stuck until the user
sent a message — which then started the load for real, hiding the cause.

Poll immediately after a preload, and show the model as loading until the
router acknowledges it: the router registers preloads asynchronously, so
even an immediate poll can still come back empty. A router predating
/router/preload now falls back to a throwaway completion, which starts the
same lazy load the first message would — only ever against a confirmed
Furiosa router, so no one's metered endpoint gets billed for it.

/model paints every model that is on a card, not just the selected one.
Holding four models across four cards is the point of the setup; without
this there was no way to see which switches are free and which mean a
two-minute cold start.

↓ from the prompt now focuses the NPU line as a footer item, where ←/→
move between the loaded models and ↵ switches. That reuses the pill
navigation already there for tasks and teammates rather than inventing a
second focus mechanism.

collapseNpuVariants dropped a variant whose base model was absent — it
counted each variant's own base as proof the base was present, so a
router advertising only `X@dp2` would have shown an empty list. It has
not bitten in production because the router always advertises the base,
but the contract said otherwise and now the code agrees.

